Depression Severity Detection Model

Overview
This project focuses on predicting depression severity levels among students using machine learning techniques. Data was collected through a Google Form that included:
- Standard GAD-7 questionnaire items
- Additional lifestyle, academic, and mental health related questions

The collected responses were preprocessed, transformed, and used to train multiple machine learning models for depression severity classification.

The framework includes:
- Data preprocessing
- Missing value handling
- Feature engineering
- Encoding categorical variables
- SMOTE for class balancing
- Model training and evaluation

Technologies Used
- Python
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Scikit-learn
- Imbalanced-learn (SMOTE)
- Joblib

Dataset Information
Data Collection
The dataset was collected using a Google Form containing:
- GAD-7 based mental health assessment questions
- Additional questions related to:
  - Academic workload
  - Sleep quality
  - Lifestyle habits
  - Placement/job worries
  - Exercise habits
  - Panic attacks
  - Social support
  - Dietary habits

Dataset Features
The dataset includes:
- Gender
- Age
- Year of Study
- Residence
- Academic Workload
- Placement/Job Worries
- Sleep Quality
- Exercise Per Week
- Alcohol Use
- Tobacco Use
- Dietary Habits
- Panic Attacks
- Loneliness
- Coping Strategies
- Sleep Hours
- Depression Score
- Depression Level

Depression Severity Classification

Depression levels were calculated using questionnaire scores and categorized into:
- Low
- Medium
- High

Data Preprocessing
The following preprocessing techniques were applied:
- Missing value handling
- Removing extra spaces from columns
- Feature transformation
- Midpoint extraction from age and sleep ranges
- Ordinal Encoding
- One-Hot Encoding
- SMOTE for handling class imbalance

Machine Learning Models Used
- Logistic Regression
- Random Forest Classifier

 Model Performance
Logistic Regression
Random Forest Classifier (Best Performing Model)

Random Forest achieved the best results after applying SMOTE and Stratified K-Fold Cross Validation.

